 ## Overview
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction is a method for generating 3D Gaussian Splatting primitives via a single feed-forward pass, typically utilizing existing multi-view generation models or global volumetric representations to achieve faithful 3D generation [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of redundant, inconsistent, and ghosting-prone primitives found in per-pixel aligned methods by predicting a global scene representation in 3D space, thereby improving global consistency and reducing artifacts in overlapping regions [S2]. Unlike standard initialization methods that rely on static Structure-from-Motion (SfM) points, this approach dynamically predicts geometry attributes (position, opacity, covariance) from a learned feature volume, often refining noisy depth estimates through iterative offset updates [S2].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction is a feed-forward method that predicts the geometric attributes (position, opacity, covariance) of 3D Gaussians from a global volumetric feature representation, rather than regressing them per-pixel from 2D image features [S2]. It solves the problem of ghosting artifacts and multi-view inconsistencies inherent in pixel-aligned Gaussian prediction methods by accumulating depth information into a unified 3D volume and refining Gaussian positions via learned offsets [S2]. Unlike standard initialization methods that use static point clouds, this approach iteratively refines geometry during the prediction phase to align Gaussians with the underlying scene structure [S2].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** A set of $N$ sparse input images $\{I_n\}_{n=1}^N$ with known camera poses (intrinsic matrices $K_n$ and extrinsic matrices $T_n = [R_n | t_n]$) and optional depth estimations $\{D_n\}_{n=1}^N$ [S2].
