 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward inference is significantly faster than iterative optimization-based 3DGS, as it avoids per-scene gradient descent. The complexity is dominated by the sparse 3D CNN feature extraction and the MLP heads [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses a global feature volume $F$ aligned with world coordinates, which scales with the scene size but avoids storing per-pixel cost volumes for every view [S2].
-*   **Measured Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering speeds for urban scenes. It improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts compared to pixel-aligned methods like MVSplat, Flash3D, and DrivingForward [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles large-scale urban scenes with sparse inputs; reduces ghosting artifacts through global volume prediction; efficient feed-forward inference [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of off-the-shelf depth estimators; may lose high-frequency appearance details if primitives are downsampled too aggressively (mitigated by IBR-based color prediction) [S2].
-*   **When to use:** Large-scale urban scene reconstruction from sparse views where real-time rendering and global consistency are prioritized over per-scene fine-tuning [S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Small-scale scenes with dense multi-view overlap where iterative optimization might yield higher fidelity; scenes where high-frequency texture details are critical and cannot be recovered via 2D context retrieval [S2].
